1
00:00:00,890 --> 00:00:04,430
Welcome to the lecture about Security in Operating
Systems.

2
00:00:04,430 --> 00:00:09,440
You can find here the reading instructions
for this lecture. Most of the material is

3
00:00:09,440 --> 00:00:14,420
covered in these slides, while other parts
are for you to read directly from the book.

4
00:00:14,420 --> 00:00:20,359
We are going to begin this lecture with an
introduction and overview of what security

5
00:00:20,359 --> 00:00:25,579
is (in the context of Operating Systems),
we will then cover the topic of Protection

6
00:00:25,579 --> 00:00:32,750
Domains and have a look at both Access Control
Lists and Capabilities. We will then discuss

7
00:00:32,750 --> 00:00:39,039
Authentication and finally cover a very well-known
way of exploiting software bugs: buffer overflow

8
00:00:39,039 --> 00:00:40,280
attacks.

9
00:00:40,280 --> 00:00:43,859
Let’s start with our overview.

10
00:00:43,859 --> 00:00:50,269
We all know that the information we access
and store in our computer is being moved across

11
00:00:50,269 --> 00:00:55,679
different devices, like the disk, the main
memory, the network card, and so on.

12
00:00:55,679 --> 00:00:59,600
We also know that the operating system has
a key role in deciding how this information

13
00:00:59,600 --> 00:01:04,930
is being moved.
It is of course clear that part of this information

14
00:01:04,930 --> 00:01:10,690
is important and/or sensitive: your passwords,
your credit card number, and so on

15
00:01:10,690 --> 00:01:17,409
and we can imagine attackers might be interested
in accessing such information. Something important

16
00:01:17,409 --> 00:01:22,070
to notice here is that such wish to access
information is not limited to reading the

17
00:01:22,070 --> 00:01:26,990
information, someone might want to alter it
(to pretend you said something in a mail you

18
00:01:26,990 --> 00:01:33,250
did not) or just prevent you (the real owner
of such information) from accessing it (so

19
00:01:33,250 --> 00:01:39,540
that you cannot recover all your files if
you do not pay a ransom for them).

20
00:01:39,540 --> 00:01:45,240
Unfortunately for us, the software we use
(including the operating system’s one) is

21
00:01:45,240 --> 00:01:52,430
full of bugs. Security bugs, called vulnerabilities,
are often used by attackers to inject special

22
00:01:52,430 --> 00:01:59,520
input (exploit) that allows them, in a way
or another, to fulfill their malicious intentions.

23
00:01:59,520 --> 00:02:06,610
At this point, it is important to realize
that one additional trade-off we have in the

24
00:02:06,610 --> 00:02:13,740
context of Operating Systems is that of feature-rich
software versus simple and secure software.

25
00:02:13,740 --> 00:02:20,519
We tend to like systems with a lot of tools,
gadgets, and features, but the truth is that

26
00:02:20,519 --> 00:02:26,160
the more of these we get, the higher the chances
that a bug can be used against us.

27
00:02:26,160 --> 00:02:33,379
If you take the very simple example about
mails, you probably agree with me that the

28
00:02:33,379 --> 00:02:39,300
possibility of sending images and files is
something we prefer over the possibility of

29
00:02:39,300 --> 00:02:44,650
sending only plain text.
But we have to acknowledge that it is hard

30
00:02:44,650 --> 00:02:49,800
for the attacker to gain control of a system
through simple text, while it is significantly

31
00:02:49,800 --> 00:02:54,640
easier if executable files can be shared via
mail.

32
00:02:54,640 --> 00:03:02,240
At this point, I want to stress that the topic
of security is a very broad and complicated

33
00:03:02,240 --> 00:03:09,290
one, and this lecture does not aim at covering
all aspects of it, of course.

34
00:03:09,290 --> 00:03:15,220
If we focus on the type of attacks and security
threats that exist, we can list (among others),

35
00:03:15,220 --> 00:03:21,629
worms: freestanding programs that can propagate
through various computers without user intervention,

36
00:03:21,629 --> 00:03:28,569
viruses, which on the other hand will attach
to files and programs and will propagate depending

37
00:03:28,569 --> 00:03:35,180
on how users interact with infected files
and programs, trojan horses, malware disguised

38
00:03:35,180 --> 00:03:40,319
as legitimate software, Denial of Service
and Distributed Denial of Service attacks,

39
00:03:40,319 --> 00:03:45,011
and so on...
If we focus on the concept of "victim", we

40
00:03:45,011 --> 00:03:50,030
must note that being a victim of a security
incident does not imply the attacker is after

41
00:03:50,030 --> 00:03:55,980
you or your data, the attacker might be using
you to achieve his or her goals.

42
00:03:55,980 --> 00:04:00,329
In this lecture, we will only focus on some
security aspects that concern the Operating

43
00:04:00,329 --> 00:04:01,370
System.

44
00:04:01,370 --> 00:04:07,879
More concretely, we will focus on
confidentiality, which is about keeping secret

45
00:04:07,879 --> 00:04:13,010
data secret or ensuring data is accessed only
by authorized people,

46
00:04:13,010 --> 00:04:18,760
integrity, which is about ensuring data is
modified only by authorized persons,

47
00:04:18,760 --> 00:04:22,790
and availability, which is about ensuring
no one can disturb the system and make it

48
00:04:22,790 --> 00:04:28,070
unusable.
As you can imagine, these are not the only

49
00:04:28,070 --> 00:04:35,480
security aspects one can think about, but
we will not cover additional ones.

50
00:04:35,480 --> 00:04:41,310
Something worth mentioning here is that we
often hear the term hacker used to refer to

51
00:04:41,310 --> 00:04:45,500
malicious attackers.
In reality, a hacker only refers to someone

52
00:04:45,500 --> 00:04:51,390
with excellent programming skills. We can
differentiate between white hats, who try

53
00:04:51,390 --> 00:04:57,030
to attack a system and develop security tools
to make such a system more secure, and black

54
00:04:57,030 --> 00:05:03,590
hats, who on the other hand use the same tools
(and hence the expression dual use) with malicious

55
00:05:03,590 --> 00:05:07,330
intentions.

56
00:05:07,330 --> 00:05:13,000
It is also worth mentioning that attacks can
be classified along different dimensions.

57
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:17,980
They could be differentiated between passive
and active ones, for instance.

58
00:05:17,980 --> 00:05:24,690
In practice, there’s no silver bullet to
handle all security aspects. We usually end

59
00:05:24,690 --> 00:05:29,690
up using a combination of various techniques,
including cryptography, software hardening

60
00:05:29,690 --> 00:05:35,630
(which we will cover later), and even more
(Intel SGX is an interesting example of a

61
00:05:35,630 --> 00:05:41,750
recent approach, which is currently a hot
research topic).

62
00:05:41,750 --> 00:05:47,040
Security practitioners usually talk of trusted
rather than secure systems. A trusted system

63
00:05:47,040 --> 00:05:53,650
is a system that has formally stated security
requirements that it meets. For a system to

64
00:05:53,650 --> 00:06:00,720
be trusted, we usually need to identify the
Trusted Computing Base, so the portion of

65
00:06:00,720 --> 00:06:07,600
hardware, kernel, and user processes with
root-level access that can affect security.

66
00:06:07,600 --> 00:06:13,770
Such portion should be kept as small as possible,
to make it easier to maintain it.

67
00:06:13,770 --> 00:06:19,610
Once we have our TCB, we need a model that
specifies what should be protected, who can

68
00:06:19,610 --> 00:06:25,580
do what, and what type of adversary we can
deal with (his/her power, basically) and make

69
00:06:25,580 --> 00:06:31,980
sure all system calls are checked by a so-called
reference monitor before entering/operating

70
00:06:31,980 --> 00:06:35,350
in the TCB.

